[Music]  it's the world's third largest industry  but that was before the covet 19  pandemic brought tourism  to a screeching halt that's been  especially hard for europe  the world's number one travel  destination in years past  tourism brought regular surpluses to  european budgets  border shutdowns and restrictions have  shown just how many jobs are dependent  on foreign visitors  and it's not just airlines and tourism  companies but also hotels  cafes and businesses all dependent on  bustling travel  but with the full coffers of the past  also came growing tensions across europe  higher living costs gentrification and  frustrations for locals in many cities  like here in barcelona but also venice  and amsterdam  will tourism ever bounce back to its  pre-pandemic highs  and could this extended off-season be an  opportunity  to rethink travel for the future  hello and welcome to deutsche welle's  coveted 19 special i'm steven beardsley  in berlin it's good to have you with us  a tourism may be a double-edged sword  for europe but the pandemic has reminded  the continent just how important the  sector is for its economy  especially for jobs john philip schultz  brings us this report from spain's  canary islands  while the season is coming to an end in  most of europe the real action is just  beginning on the spanish island of  fuerteventura  italian petra uber lehner has lived here  for 15 years  she runs a kitesurfing school after  months of coronavirus restrictions  she's now hoping for winter vacationers  the subtropical climate here is pleasant  in the colder months and it's nearly  always windy  i hear they should arrive again flights  and also we  we have a lot of reservations i mean not  like  for long term or more like asking for  information how the situation is looking  like but now there are there's a lot of  people from all over europe  interested in coming here and i hope it  can be possible for everybody  victor cobelo is far less optimistic he  runs a small country hotel in the  interior of the island  the restaurant is doing reasonably well  but he says the hotel business has  collapsed because for months people  all over the world have been warned  against travel to spain  he has already had to let go several  employees on his small team  and he sees few signs of improvement  about two-thirds of all workers on  fuerteventura live from tourism  the crisis is sitting the african and  south american migrant workers on the  island particularly hard  many lost their jobs from one day to the  next and do not appear in any official  statistics  hundreds of migrants work without papers  in the hotels and restaurants on the  island  according to local aid organizations  unlike other residents  they are not entitled to any government  support and for them  the absence of tourists is becoming more  and more of an existential threat  the island government doesn't even try  to put a gloss on things  it is now pinning all its hopes on a  meeting of the eu tourism ministers at  the end of the month  the government commissioners hopes rest  on travel corridors  his government's idea on arrival and  departure the island could test  all tourists for the corona virus and  existing quarantine regulations could be  lifted  i hope in that meeting 28th of september  in europe they are able to say okay all  european countries decide  how to proceed to make those tests in  the airport  on the port the government of the  canaries and also for toronto  we have the budget we have the money to  make it possible  they could start airport tests as early  as tomorrow the politicians said  it would be expensive but it would be  worth it if planes finally landed on the  island  regularly again so the view there from  spain's canary islands  let's look at the broader woes facing  global tourism  the pain started in the first three  months of the years  of the year rather as asia locked down  and that led to a 22 percent pullback in  the sector  and while those numbers have improved  slightly in some regions the overall  tourism industry is looking at a  whopping 60 to 80 percent contraction  for the full year  and that would put as many as 120  million  direct tourism jobs at risk  and let's talk now to richard weston  he's a senior research fellow at the  lancashire school of business in the uk  where he focuses on tourism richard it's  good to have you on the show  how dependent has europe become on  tourism  uh yeah i think that um  probably as a region europe isn't more  dependent than lots of other regions in  the world but  there are parts of europe itself  particularly spain and italy greece  which have become very dependent on on  tourism perhaps uh  15 of their gdp comes from from just  this one sector that's staggering what  does that mean for jobs  they have the job that's a a lot of jobs  and of course it's not just the  the directly employed people in in the  in the industry but all the ancillary  support staff you know the  the um the travel companies and and the  laundry companies and everybody that  does anything that might be connected to  tourism so the supply chains  are also largely impacted you could  probably add 50  again to that figure what is that  well let me ask first at the same time  you have all these jobs but then  the old business model in some ways  wasn't that great a lot of these cities  amsterdam venice barcelona really  chafing at the amount of tourists that  were coming through their cities  um and what it had done culturally what  it had done to gentrification things  like that  certainly yeah some of the cities have  become  far too successful if you like  and this is now causing problems for the  local  populations the resident population in  terms of  increasing property prices and  local shops closing because there aren't  enough local buyers  so some some places and i know barcelona  in particular has started to address  this  in terms of of the attract and disperse  model where they  they're not trying to put people off but  once they arrive then trying to push  them out into the wider region  um but he career there are lots of  places in in europe which were  clamoring for more tourists you know  some of the  the um the new member states in in the  balkans and places like that  uh where they they were under tourists  rather than over tourists you know  well now it looks like everyone is under  tourists how long can we expect things  to last as they are how long will this  industry be down  right how long is a piece of string  um that's a  a very good question i i think certainly  um this year will be written off um  we're already seeing a second spike and  so i think you know that  we're coming towards the end of the  primary tourist  season anyway but but i think you know  we're seeing this second spike we're  likely to get more local lockdowns  more interruptions to travel more  borders closing  uh more quarantine periods so all of  these things will have a  negative impact on on tourism  um i i think the long-term impact is  likely to be felt  for for many years perhaps up to a  decade  and it just does depend on on  the progress of of the virus and whether  we have a  vaccine if we don't have a vaccine an  effective one  then that means that we will be looking  forward to social distancing  for uh the foreseeable future  and of course that has an impact on the  capacity of hotels and restaurants  cafes beaches even um and so that's  going to have a  a serious impact on certainly the mass  market  so the the places that were relying  heavily  on tourism for those areas were that  attracted tourists but they were more  dispersed  then obviously it's less of an issue  because there's that  natural spacing going on between people  all right richard weston joining us from  the university of lancashire thank you  very much  my pleasure and once again it's time for  your questions answered by our science  correspondent derek williams  [Music]  do we need to stop taking medications  for high blood pressure  do they make covid19 worse  this is one of those questions that's  been subjected to a lot of intense  scrutiny over the months  early on in this pandemic there was a  lot of worry among  health care professionals that certain  medications called  ace2 inhibitors which are used to lower  blood pressure  that they might actually make people  more susceptible  to covet 19 that's because we think they  might increase  levels of what's called angiotensin  converting  enzyme to or or ace2 in your cell  membranes  and we confirmed at an early stage that  that just like with  other coronaviruses the ace2 receptor is  kind of the door knob  that that sars co2 uses to to open the  door  to your cells so the theory was that  having more ace2 receptors would  probably be a bad thing  however stopping blood pressure  medication suddenly can also have  serious side effects so the medical  advice was  keep taking your meds until we know more  well fast forward six months and and we  do know more and guess what  it turns out the opposite could be true  um a recent  large-scale meta-analysis of of 19 other  studies  conducted by british researchers shows  that taking those  hypertension medications doesn't put you  at greater risk of a severe coveting  outcome  in fact the study says they might  actually reduce the risk  of disease severity although that wasn't  proven definitively but really  i think whether they actually help or  not is almost beside the point um  just knowing that we're now pretty sure  ace2 inhibitors really  don't make covet 19 worse will be a big  load off the minds of the people who  take them  and the doctors who prescribe them as  well  [Music]  all right our science correspondent  derek williams there and finally here's  a special flight for  australian travelers with nowhere in  particular to go  qantas is offering a seven-hour scenic  flight called  great southern land ground australia on  october 10th  it will allow passengers to catch a  glimpse of popular attractions such as  luru the great barrier reef and sydney  harbour  tickets cost 570 us dollars apiece and  sold out in just a few minutes  you

